Q(TAG_RNA, "Influenza B versus influenza A",
  "Public health surveillance data show that one influenza type causes seasonal epidemics but has never caused a pandemic and disproportionately affects children, while the other type undergoes reassortment and has caused pandemics. Which statement correctly distinguishes the two?",
  ["Influenza B does not undergo antigenic shift because it lacks an animal reservoir",
   "Influenza A causes milder disease overall and mainly affects school-age children each year",
   "Influenza B has caused every major pandemic recorded in the last century",
   "Influenza A cannot undergo antigenic drift, only antigenic shift",
   "Influenza B has an equally broad range of animal reservoirs as influenza A"],
  "Influenza A infects a wide range of animal hosts, including birds and pigs, which allows reassortment (antigenic shift) and has produced every influenza pandemic. Influenza B circulates mainly in humans and lacks this animal reservoir, so it undergoes only antigenic drift and never shift, and it tends to disproportionately affect children while generally causing less severe epidemics than influenza A.",
  difficulty="medium",
  why={"Influenza A causes milder disease overall and mainly affects": "Influenza A is generally associated with more severe disease and broader age impact, and influenza B is the one more associated with pediatric predominance.",
       "Influenza B has caused every major pandemic recorded in the last": "Every recorded pandemic has been caused by influenza A, not influenza B.",
       "Influenza A cannot undergo antigenic drift, only antigenic shift": "Influenza A undergoes both drift and shift; it is influenza B that is limited to drift alone.",
       "Influenza B has an equally broad range of animal reservoirs as": "Influenza B lacks the extensive animal reservoirs of influenza A, which is exactly why it cannot undergo shift."},
  table=None),

