Q(TAG_RNA, "Measles: giant cell pneumonia in immunocompromised patients",
  "A child with acute lymphoblastic leukemia develops progressive respiratory failure and diffuse infiltrates on chest imaging weeks after a known measles exposure, without ever developing the typical rash. Lung biopsy shows multinucleated giant cells with viral inclusions. What does this represent?",
  ["Severe measles pneumonitis occurring in a patient with no rash",
   "A typical case of measles with the rash simply overlooked by staff",
   "Superimposed bacterial pneumonia unrelated to the measles exposure",
   "A hypersensitivity pneumonitis triggered by chemotherapy exposure",
   "Cytomegalovirus pneumonitis coincidentally occurring at the same time"],
  "Severely immunocompromised patients, particularly those with impaired cell-mediated immunity such as leukemia patients, can develop measles giant cell (Hecht) pneumonia, a severe and often fatal pneumonitis with multinucleated giant cells containing viral inclusions, frequently without the typical rash because the exanthem depends on an intact immune response. This atypical, rashless presentation can delay recognition of measles as the cause.",
  difficulty="hard",
  why={"A typical case of measles with the rash simply overlooked": "The rash is often genuinely absent in this immunocompromised presentation rather than merely missed by observers.",
       "Superimposed bacterial pneumonia unrelated to the measles": "The biopsy finding of multinucleated giant cells with viral inclusions points to direct measles pneumonitis, not a separate bacterial process.",
       "A hypersensitivity pneumonitis triggered by chemotherapy": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis would not show giant cells with viral inclusions on biopsy.",
       "Cytomegalovirus pneumonitis coincidentally occurring at the": "CMV can cause pneumonitis in immunocompromised hosts, but the scenario's known exposure and biopsy findings point specifically to measles."},
  table=None),

