Q(TAG_DNA, "Molluscum contagiosum: Henderson-Patterson bodies",
  "A biopsy of a dome-shaped, umbilicated skin papule shows keratinocytes containing large eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions that displace the nucleus to the periphery. Which organism produces this histologic finding?",
  ["Molluscum contagiosum virus",
   "Human papillomavirus",
   "Varicella-zoster virus",
   "Herpes simplex virus",
   "Staphylococcus aureus"],
  "Molluscum contagiosum virus, a poxvirus, produces large cytoplasmic inclusion bodies called Henderson-Patterson (molluscum) bodies within infected keratinocytes, visible as eosinophilic material that pushes the nucleus to the cell periphery. Clinically this produces small, flesh-colored, dome-shaped papules with central umbilication, most common in children and in immunocompromised or sexually active adults.",
  difficulty="easy",
  why={"Human papillomavirus": "HPV produces koilocytes with perinuclear halos, not the discrete cytoplasmic inclusion bodies described here.",
       "Varicella-zoster virus": "VZV produces multinucleated giant cells with Cowdry type A intranuclear inclusions, not cytoplasmic Henderson-Patterson bodies.",
       "Herpes simplex virus": "HSV also produces multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions rather than the large cytoplasmic bodies described.",
       "Staphylococcus aureus": "A bacterial organism would not produce a characteristic intracytoplasmic viral inclusion body on histology."},
  table=None),
